outputs.exact_statement:
On the cone of matrices positive definite on a fixed support, $\Phi$ is smooth. For Hermitian directions $K,L$ on that support, $$d\Phi_P(K)=\frac32\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(\sqrt P K),
 \qquad
 d^2\Phi_P(K,L)=\mathcal B_P(K,L).$$ If $p_i>0$ are the eigenvalues on the support, then in an orthonormal eigenbasis $$\label{eq:hessian-metric}
 b_P(K)=\frac32\sum_{i,j}
          \frac{|K_{ij}|^2}{\sqrt{p_i}+\sqrt{p_j}}.$$ In particular, $\mathcal B_P$ is a positive definite symmetric bilinear form on the real space of supported Hermitian directions, and $$|\mathcal B_P(K,L)|\le b_P(K)^{1/2}b_P(L)^{1/2}.$$

Let $R:E\to F$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional complex Hilbert spaces, and let $L:F\to F$ be Hermitian. The matrix $R^*LR$ is supported on $R^*R$, and $$\label{eq:insertion-bound}
 b_{R^*R}(R^*LR)
 \le\frac34\lVert L\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}^2\Phi(R^*R).$$

On either of these measure spaces, write $M$ for the corresponding centered maximal operator. For $f\in L^1$ and $\rho>0$, $$\mu\{Mf>\rho\}\le\frac3\rho\|f\|_1.$$ For every $1<q<\infty$ and $f\in L^q$, $$\label{eq:line-maximal-strong}
 \|Mf\|_q^q\le
 \frac{6q\,2^{q-1}}{q-1}\|f\|_q^q.$$ Moreover, there is an absolute constant $C_g$ such that, whenever $h>0$, $\sigma\ge h^2$ and $n\in\mathbb Z$, $$\label{eq:discrete-gaussian-maximal}
 \sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}h g_\sigma((m-n)h)|f(m)|
 \le C_g M_{\mathbb Z}f(n).$$

For finite rectangular complex matrices $R_-,R_+$ of the same size, put $R(z)=(1-z)R_-+zR_+$ and $D=R_+-R_-$. Then $$\label{eq:rank-change-derivative}
 \begin{aligned}
 &\Phi(R_+^*R_+)-\Phi(R_-^*R_-)\\
 &\quad=3\int_0^1\operatorname{Re}
   \langle R(z)\sqrt{R(z)^*R(z)},D\rangle_{\mathrm{HS}}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dz.
 \end{aligned}$$ For every rectangular matrix $R$ and every entry $(i,j)$, $$\label{eq:entry-gradient}
 |(R\sqrt{R^*R})_{ij}|
 \le\|\operatorname{row}_iR\|_2\,
       \|\operatorname{col}_jR\|_2.$$

For every $h>0$, $N\ge0$, complex arrays $A_v$ on $\{-N,\ldots,N\}^2$, and endpoint choices as above, let $\alpha$ be any permutation of $(1,11/10,11/10)$ and use variances $\sigma_v=\alpha_vs$. The corresponding masked costs satisfy $$\label{eq:masked-dissipation}
 \int_{s_-}^{s_+}\int_{\Pi_0}
       \sum_{v=0}^2 V_v(p,s)\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\pi\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm ds
 \le C\mathcal S(A).$$ The constant is independent of the arrays, the mesh, the endpoints, and the number of distinct endpoint values.

For all complex $F,G\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^2)$, $$\left\|\sup_{0<\varepsilon<R}
          |B^I_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)|\right\|_{L^{3/2}(\mathbb R^2)}
 \le C\|F\|_{L^3(\mathbb R^2)}\|G\|_{L^3(\mathbb R^2)}.$$ The supremum is measurable and the constant is absolute.

There are an absolute constant $C$ and functions $E_r$, $r>0$, such that, for $t\ne0$, apart from endpoint values, $$\label{eq:maximal-hard-decomposition}
 I_{\varepsilon,R}(t)
  =\frac{c_0}{t}\mathbf1_{\{\varepsilon<|t|<R\}}
       +E_\varepsilon(t)-E_R(t),
 \qquad c_0=-\frac{2}{3\sqrt{6\pi}},$$ and $$\label{eq:maximal-error-envelope}
 |E_r(t)|\le\frac{C}{r}\left(1+\frac{|t|}{r}\right)^{-2}
 \qquad(t\ne0).$$

For complex $F,G\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, there is a set of full measure on which every finite truncation is absolutely convergent and depends continuously on $(\varepsilon,R)$ in $0<\varepsilon<R<\infty$. The resulting family is independent of the representatives of $F,G$ up to a common null set. Moreover, $$\label{eq:elementary-bilinear}
 \left\|\int_{\varepsilon<|t|<R}
 |F(x+t,y)G(x,y+t)|\,\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm dt}{|t|}\right\|_p
 \le 2\log(R/\varepsilon)\|F\|_3\|G\|_3.$$

For all complex $F,G\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, the joint limit $$B(F,G)=\lim_{\substack{\varepsilon\downarrow0\\R\uparrow\infty}}
            B_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)$$ exists almost everywhere and in $L^{3/2}(\mathbb R^2)$, with $\|B(F,G)\|_{3/2}\le C_M\|F\|_3\|G\|_3$. More precisely, $$\label{eq:maximal-tail-convergence}
 \left\|\sup_{\substack{0<\varepsilon<1/n\\R>n}}
 |B_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)-B(F,G)|\right\|_{3/2}
 \longrightarrow0.$$ The limit is the unique bounded bilinear extension of the principal-value operator on compactly supported smooth inputs.

For complex $F_0,F_1,F_2\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, every finite flat truncation is absolutely integrable, and $$\sup_{0<\varepsilon<R<\infty}
 |\mathcal L_{\varepsilon,R}(F_0,F_1,F_2)|
 \le C_M\prod_{v=0}^2\|F_v\|_3.$$ Its joint scalar principal value exists and equals $\int F_0 B(F_1,F_2)$. This limiting form is the unique bounded trilinear extension of its compact smooth definition.

For all complex $G_0,G_1,G_2\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, every finite simplex truncation is absolutely integrable, and $$\sup_{0<\varepsilon<R<\infty}
 |\Lambda_{\varepsilon,R}(G_0,G_1,G_2)|
 \le C_M\prod_{v=0}^2\|G_v\|_3.$$ Its joint scalar principal value exists for every such triple and is the unique bounded trilinear extension of its compact smooth definition. The optimal constants in the uniform flat and simplex scalar estimates are equal.
